\title{Owner's design notes (verbatim, in order received)}
\date{2026-11-24}
\author{claude-fable-5-2}
\contributor{queclr}
\tag{policy}
\meta{origin}{docs/design/OWNER-NOTES.md}
\meta{moved}{2026-11-24}
\import{base-macros}

\p{These are the project owner's own steering comments, collected by the orchestrator so that every agent sees them. Where a research document or a design draft conflicts with them, the notes win; say what following them costs. The "Reading" lines are the orchestrator's interpretation, not the owner's words.}

\ol{
\li{\p{"notice that the leader board time is virtual clock and it is reported that nano-lean leading does not reflect wall clock"}
\p{Reading: optimise and report both instruction count (board rank) and wall/CPU time.}}
\li{\p{"2.06 s with SerialGC, 7.46 s with ParallelGC and 10.0 s with G1: notice that realistic workload requires parallelism"}
\p{Reading: runtime and GC choices are evaluated with parallel workers and wall clock, never from single-threaded CPU totals. Parallel workers are the design point.}}
\li{\p{"notice that depending on your 'core elaboration language design', the NbE VM can be pretty hot."}
\p{Reading: the language that constants and terms are elaborated into is the central design artifact; the machine should carry essentially all of the checking work.}}
\li{\p{"I am thinking that the whole project is like a mechanization of lean kernel tyck. similar to other VM-like mechanization work." / "I mean 'mechanization' in real type theory context. e.g. A mechanical formalization of higher-ranked polymorphic type inference. It is like building a VM for DK."}
\p{Reading: the worklist formulation (Zhao, Oliveira, Schrijvers, ICFP 2019): judgments are worklist entries, inference judgments carry continuations, the algorithm is a first-order small-step transition system. RLVM is the machine that steps it for the Lean kernel.}}
\li{\p{"I don't think lambda-calculus is the language for truffle. truffle jit should be for the mechanized check progress. not the language itself."}
\p{Reading: the Truffle guest language is the judgment/instruction language; lambda terms and values are its data.}}
\li{\p{"this is like developing an instruction set for lean4 NbE and def_eq. which sounds exciting"}}
\li{\p{"notice that nat itself is a good thing to specialize: e.g. native integer for small range ..."}
\p{Reading: Nat gets first-class specialisation in the instruction set and value model, not only a literal-arithmetic library: small naturals as immediate (unboxed) values that never touch the value store, arithmetic/comparison instructions with a native fast path and an exact overflow guard that falls to the big-number path, cheap views between literal and \code{\verb~|Nat.succ~}/\code{\verb~|Nat.zero~} constructor forms (offsets), \code{\verb~|Nat.rec~}/\code{\verb~|casesOn~} on a literal in one step. In the Truffle tier this is the classic type specialisation (long with overflow check, rewrite to big on overflow).}}
\li{\p{"meanwhile some data structure specialization may also be interesting, like how graaljs specialize their array, but this is more like an open design question"}
\p{Reading (open question, to be explored with measurements, not committed): storage strategies in the GraalJS sense - a value keeps a specialised representation while its contents allow it and transitions to a more general one when they do not. Candidates: application spines and captured frames (inline small / flat / chunked), constructor cells by arity (nullary as immediates), \code{\verb~|List~}/\code{\verb~|Array~}/\code{\verb~|String~}/\code{\verb~|Char~}/\code{\verb~|BitVec~}/\code{\verb~|Fin~}/\code{\verb~|UIntN~} values that stay packed until a recursor or projection needs the constructor view. Any strategy that short-cuts a library definition is only sound if the definition is verified to have the expected shape, exactly like the Nat and String literal prerequisites. The value model should leave room for strategy tags and transitions.}}
\li{\p{"You need to check Cedar level workload. Init may not be interesting enough"}
\p{Reading: heat and JIT economics must be judged on realistic project workloads (the arena's cedar, con-leche, reachability, navier-stokes-euler, cslib corpora and mathlib), where the kernel really reduces, not on Init/Std. A dynamic workload characterisation is in docs/research/13 (in progress).}}
\li{\p{"We don't want to beat instructions. care more about CPU time plz."}
\p{Reading: THE PRIMARY METRIC IS CPU TIME (task-clock summed over all threads), then wall clock with parallel workers. Instruction counts are still recorded because the arena's board uses them, but they are not the optimisation target. Consequences: prefer cache-friendly designs even when they execute more instructions (compact records, locality, fewer random probes into large tables, matching against a pattern instead of building and interning); budgets and governors (compilation, speculation) are expressed in CPU time or steps, not in billed instructions; the platform choice (JVM with JIT versus native image) is decided by measured CPU time and wall clock; every benchmark table lists CPU time first. Where ISA.md or ARCHITECTURE.md argue from instruction counts, re-read the argument with CPU time in mind.}}
\li{\p{(2026-11-24, answering four open questions) "1. resemble official by default 2. do not download EE on default, but you can report by here 3. commit each step, I need history. 4. you can increase to 32 agents a time. profile more and optimize more."}
\p{Reading:}
\ul{
\li{Universe levels: BOTH engines compare universe levels as the official kernel does by default (its incomplete algorithm); the complete relation is an opt-in switch only. The two arena corner cases \code{\verb~|imax-right-successor~} and \code{\verb~|imax-nested-absorption~} must be rejected by default, as the official checker rejects them.}
\li{Oracle GraalVM ("EE", GFTC licence): the default build, the arena build and every default script must not download or use it. It may be used for measurements that are reported to the owner, clearly labelled; nothing that ships depends on it.}
\li{Version control: the orchestrator commits every step (agents still do not run git commands).}
\li{Team size: up to 32 agents at a time; the extra seats go to profiling and optimisation.}
}}
\li{\p{(2026-11-24) "as I said, instruction cost is what arena chasing, not us. wall clock time after warmup/pipelined is our goal. we care about 'real checking performance'"}
\p{Reading (this refines note 10 and replaces every "CPU time first" in earlier documents and briefs):}
\ul{
\li{THE GOAL IS REAL CHECKING PERFORMANCE: wall-clock time of checking with parallel workers, measured in steady state, i.e. after warm-up (JIT compilation, table growth, page faults of the first seconds) and with loading pipelined with checking. Instruction counts are the arena's metric, not ours: they are recorded for information only. CPU time is a secondary diagnostic (it tells how much parallelism is left), not the target.}
\li{Warm-up cost is not charged. Consequences: a JIT (HotSpot C2 or Graal on the JVM launcher, and the Truffle tier for the machine's code objects) is judged by the steady-state speed it reaches, not by what compiling costs; background compiler threads on spare cores are fine; promotion thresholds can be low; the platform question (JVM with JIT against native image) is decided by warm wall clock.}
\li{Every benchmark reports, in this order: warm wall clock with 8 workers (pipelined), warm wall clock with 1 worker, cold wall clock of a fresh process (for information), CPU time, peak RSS, instructions. "Warm" = the same process checks the same export again after a first pass (per-pass times reported), or the steady-state rate after the first N seconds of a long corpus.}
\li{Correctness rules are unchanged, and the arena's interface (run line, exit codes, official verdicts) is still honoured; only the optimisation target changed.}
}}
\li{\p{(2026-11-24, answering the question "strict or practical exactness for the default?") "practical"}
\p{The question as it was put to the owner: adversarial reviews found that the official kernel's verdict can depend on its cache order, on the non-transitivity of its comparison and on whether a subterm is one export record or two; on such files the machine (and in three cases the reference engine) accepts a statement the official checker rejects, every such statement is true in the theory, and no false accept was found. Strict = the default matches the official checker on every input (then the reference engine stays the default and the machine is an opt-in fast mode). Practical = official verdicts on all real inputs, never a false accept, and accepting true statements that the official checker rejects for reasons internal to its implementation is tolerated, as the leading checkers do; the reference engine stays available as the strict mode; the two proof-comparison shortcuts become admissible as a profile.}
\p{Reading (this refines note 11, item 1, which stays as it is for universe levels):}
\ul{
\li{THE CONTRACT OF THE DEFAULT CONFIGURATION. (a) Never accept a statement that is not derivable in Lean's type theory ("kind F"): absolute, and the first thing every review attacks. (b) The official verdict on every real input: everything lean4export writes (the corpora), the arena's good and bad tests, the mutant corpora with their stored official verdicts. (c) Everything the official checker accepts is accepted (a rejection is always confirmed by the reference engine; a decline goes to it). (d) Accepting a derivable statement that the official checker rejects only through its own incompleteness, non-transitivity, cache order or object identity ("kind T") is TOLERATED; the known classes are written down with one export each (docs/design/review/official-divergence.md).}
\li{The machine may be the default engine once (a)-(c) are shown by the gates and an adversarial review of the candidate has found no kind F. The proof-comparison shortcuts (skipargs, congruence; review docs/design/review/soundness-shortcuts.md: theory-sound) are admissible; whether they are on by default is decided by warm wall clock over all corpora (they cost on con-leche).}
\li{THE STRICT MODE stays: the reference engine, kept as close to the official implementation as we can make it (declining where it would need the official object identity). One launcher option selects the policy; behaviour that exists only for bug-for-bug agreement on adversarial files (declining metadata nodes, typed bindings if they cost time) belongs to the strict mode, not to the default.}
}}
\li{\p{(2026-11-24, on publishing the repository) "License: I hope we can use Apache-2.0/MIT for code with GNU doc for journal if applicable. I am open to accept oracle graal CE license to run CI."}
\p{Reading:}
\ul{
\li{The code is dual-licensed Apache-2.0 OR MIT, at a user's option (file LICENSE at the repository root, in the form the owner's other project uses). The journal (\code{\verb~|journal/~}) is documentation under the GNU Free Documentation License 1.3 or later (\code{\verb~|journal/LICENSE~}).}
\li{Continuous integration may download and run GraalVM Community Edition (GPL v2 with the Classpath exception). Note 11, item 2 is unchanged: Oracle GraalVM (GFTC) is not downloaded or used by anything default.}
}}
\li{\p{(2026-11-24) "after finalization, please move all notes to forester except readme: current the repo contains too many notes scattered everywhere. updates AGENTS.md to record requirement."}
\p{Reading: every note of the project lives in the journal (\code{\verb~|journal/~}, the Forester forest); outside it only README files remain (plus LICENSE and the agent-instruction files). Recorded in the root \code{\verb~|AGENTS.md~}. The existing documents (\code{\verb~|docs/~}, the modules' NOTES files and the like) are moved into the journal once the work in flight is finished; nothing new is added outside the journal meanwhile.}}
\li{\p{(2026-11-24) "the package also needs to improve: I expect a final single entry executable with fastest default. Make GraalVM EE with preload profile optional. I accept the license locally for experiment purpose. we have no commercial implication on the use of graal EE."}
\p{Reading:}
\ul{
\li{The release is ONE entry executable, \code{\verb~|rlvm~}, whose behaviour without any option is the fastest configuration there is (engine, switch set, load mode, worker count, memory settings): a user does not choose between launchers or switch sets.}
\li{A build with Oracle GraalVM ("EE") and a shipped profile (profile-guided optimisation: the profile of a training run is part of the repository, so that the build needs no training) is an OPTION of the build, never its default. The maintainer accepts Oracle GraalVM's licence on the development machine for experiments; the project has no commercial use of it. This relaxes note 11, item 2 to: nothing DEFAULT downloads or uses Oracle GraalVM; an explicitly requested build may.}
}}
}
